---
permalink: /page/status.html
layout: base.njk
pageTitle: Status
pageDescription: What the generator produced, what it had to guess, and what it could not find.
---
{#
  status.njk — the build's own report card.

  It opens with what the build produced, because that is the first thing worth
  knowing and the rest of the page never says it: the generator is built never
  to stop, so a page of findings alone can't tell you whether a site came out
  of it. Everything after the green tile is the list of what had to be inferred
  or defaulted, so you can fix what matters and ignore what doesn't.

  Link and file-size checking lives in healthcheck.sh / healthcheck.ps1, which
  run against the finished HTML.

  Findings are ranked low / medium / high — yellow, orange, red — by how visible
  the problem is in the built site. The rank is decided in buildHealthReport()
  in lib/library.js, never here; this file only paints what it is handed. Each
  colour carries a ●/●●/●●● marker so the ranking still reads in greyscale, and
  the green tile carries a ✓ for the same reason.
#}
{% set report = library.report %}
{% set health = library.health %}
{% set pages = library.stats.pages %}

{#
  findingPath — a finding's path, as a link when there is somewhere to go.

  `pageUrl` is passed in explicitly and is NOT optional: Nunjucks macros do not
  inherit the caller's context, so the `rel` filter cannot reach `page.url` from
  in here and would silently resolve every href against "/". card-grid.njk has
  the same note for the same reason.

  Opens in a new tab on purpose — the point of the report is working down a list
  of findings, and following one in place loses your place in thirty rows.

  A row with no url renders exactly as it did before links existed, which is
  what the settings section and any unresolvable finding fall back to.
#}
{% macro findingPath(text, url, pageUrl) %}
{%- if url -%}
<a class="health-item__path health-item__path--link" href="{{ url | rel(pageUrl) }}"
   target="_blank" rel="noopener">{{ text }}</a>
{%- else -%}
<code class="health-item__path">{{ text }}</code>
{%- endif -%}
{% endmacro %}

<div class="page-container pt-28 nav-desktop:pt-[5.6rem] pb-16 extra_fade_effect">

  <header class="section-header">
    <p class="section-header__kicker">Report</p>
    <h1 class="section-header__title">{{ "Status" | animateTitle | safe }}</h1>
    <p class="section-header__meta">
      {{ library.stats.variants }} documents · {{ report.cleanFiles }} fully annotated ·
      {% if report.worst %}<span class="health-sev health-sev--{{ report.worst }}">{{ report.totalFindings }} findings</span>{% else %}{{ report.totalFindings }} findings{% endif %}
    </p>
  </header>

  <div class="health-summary">
    {# Not a link, unlike every tile beside it: there is no section to jump to,
       because a page that rendered has nothing to say for itself. #}
    <div class="health-tile health-tile--ok">
      <span class="health-tile__count">{{ pages.ok }}</span>
      <span class="health-tile__label">
        <span class="health-mark">✓</span>Pages rendered
      </span>
      <span class="health-tile__note">
        {{ pages.documents }} documents · {{ pages.folders }} folders ·
        {{ pages.facets }} tag pages · {{ pages.site }} site pages
      </span>
    </div>

    {% for section in report.sections %}
      {# A section with nothing in it is nothing to focus on: it stays grey. #}
      <a class="health-tile{{ ' health-tile--zero' if section.count == 0 else ' health-tile--' + section.severity }}" href="#{{ section.key }}">
        <span class="health-tile__count">{{ section.count }}</span>
        <span class="health-tile__label">
          {% if section.count %}<span class="health-mark">{{ section.mark }}</span>{% endif %}{{ section.label }}
        </span>
        <span class="health-tile__note">{{ section.note }}</span>
      </a>
    {% endfor %}
  </div>

  {# ── Failed to render ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     First because it is the only finding on this page that means a document is
     missing from the site rather than merely thin: the renderer crashed, the
     page was still written, and its body is a one-line apology. #}
  {% set sev = report.severity.render %}
  <section class="health-section" id="render">
    <h2 class="health-section__title{{ ' health-section__title--' + sev.level if health.render.length }}">
      {% if health.render.length %}<span class="health-mark">{{ sev.mark }}</span>{% endif %}Failed to render
    </h2>
    {% if health.render.length %}
      <p class="health-section__lede">
        The build did not stop, and the page exists — but everything these files
        had to say is gone from it. This is the one thing on this page worth
        fixing before anything else.
      </p>
      <ul class="health-list">
        {% for row in health.render %}
          <li class="health-item health-item--{{ sev.level }}">
            {{ findingPath(row.file, row.url, page.url) }}
            <span class="health-item__note">{{ row.message }}</span>
          </li>
        {% endfor %}
      </ul>
    {% else %}
      <p class="health-ok">Every document rendered.</p>
    {% endif %}
  </section>

  {# ── Settings ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     Second, and for the same reason render is first: a setting that did not
     take means the site is not the site that was asked for, and nothing else
     anywhere would ever mention it. A missing settings file is not a finding —
     the defaults are a perfectly good site. #}
  {% set sev = report.severity.settings %}
  <section class="health-section" id="settings">
    <h2 class="health-section__title{{ ' health-section__title--' + sev.level if health.settings.length }}">
      {% if health.settings.length %}<span class="health-mark">{{ sev.mark }}</span>{% endif %}Settings
    </h2>
    {% if health.settings.length %}
      <p class="health-section__lede">
        These values in <code>site_settings.json</code> could not be used, so the
        built-in default was applied instead. The build was never at risk; the
        site simply is not what the file asked for.
      </p>
      <ul class="health-list">
        {% for row in health.settings %}
          <li class="health-item health-item--{{ sev.level }}">
            <code class="health-item__path">{{ row.field }}</code>
            <span class="health-item__note">{{ row.message }}</span>
          </li>
        {% endfor %}
      </ul>
    {% elif library.stats.settingsFound %}
      <p class="health-ok">
        Every setting in <code>site_settings.json</code> was applied
        ({{ library.stats.itemsPerPage }} cards per page).
      </p>
    {% else %}
      <p class="health-ok">
        No <code>site_settings.json</code> — the built-in defaults were used
        ({{ library.stats.itemsPerPage }} cards per page). Create one at
        <code>{{ library.stats.settingsFile }}</code> to set the name,
        navigation and page size.
      </p>
    {% endif %}
  </section>

  {# ── Metadata ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── #}
  {# The only section ranked per row rather than per section: one file can hold
     a missing author (low) and no frontmatter at all (high) at once. #}
  {% set sev = report.severity.metadata %}
  <section class="health-section" id="metadata">
    <h2 class="health-section__title{{ ' health-section__title--' + sev.level if report.byFile.length }}">
      {% if report.byFile.length %}<span class="health-mark">{{ sev.mark }}</span>{% endif %}Metadata
    </h2>
    {% if report.byFile.length %}
      <p class="health-section__lede">
        These files build and display correctly. Each one below simply had a field
        the generator filled in for it.
      </p>

      <ul class="health-tally">
        {% for row in report.byField %}
          <li class="health-tally__row--{{ row.severity }}">
            <span class="health-tally__count">{{ row.count }}</span> {{ row.label }}
          </li>
        {% endfor %}
      </ul>

      <ul class="health-list">
        {% for file in report.byFile %}
        <li class="health-item health-item--{{ file.severity }}">
          {{ findingPath(file.file, file.url, page.url) }}
          <ul class="health-item__detail">
            {% for warning in file.warnings %}
              <li>
                <span class="health-field health-field--{{ warning.severity }}"><span class="health-mark">{{ warning.mark }}</span>{{ warning.field }}</span>
                {{ warning.message }}
              </li>
            {% endfor %}
          </ul>
        </li>
        {% endfor %}
      </ul>
    {% else %}
      <p class="health-ok">Every document declares every field.</p>
    {% endif %}
  </section>

  {# ── Structure ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── #}
  {% set sev = report.severity.structure %}
  <section class="health-section" id="structure">
    <h2 class="health-section__title{{ ' health-section__title--' + sev.level if health.structure.length }}">
      {% if health.structure.length %}<span class="health-mark">{{ sev.mark }}</span>{% endif %}Structure
    </h2>
    {% if health.structure.length %}
      <p class="health-section__lede">
        No page is written for these, and nothing links to them. A folder with
        empty folders inside it is named once, here, rather than once per level.
      </p>
      <ul class="health-list">
        {% for row in health.structure %}
          <li class="health-item health-item--{{ sev.level }}">
            {{ findingPath(row.path, row.url, page.url) }}
            <span class="health-item__note">{{ row.message }}</span>
          </li>
        {% endfor %}
      </ul>
    {% else %}
      <p class="health-ok">Every folder produces at least one page.</p>
    {% endif %}
  </section>

  {# ── Unreferenced media ───────────────────────────────────────────────── #}
  {% set sev = report.severity.media %}
  <section class="health-section" id="media">
    <h2 class="health-section__title{{ ' health-section__title--' + sev.level if health.unreferenced.length }}">
      {% if health.unreferenced.length %}<span class="health-mark">{{ sev.mark }}</span>{% endif %}Unreferenced media
    </h2>
    {% if health.unreferenced.length %}
      <p class="health-section__lede">
        These sit in folders with no markdown, so no page links to them.
        They are not copied anywhere — they just take up space in your project.
      </p>
      <ul class="health-list health-list--compact">
        {% for row in health.unreferenced %}
          <li class="health-item health-item--{{ sev.level }}">
            {{ findingPath(row.path, row.url, page.url) }}
            <span class="health-item__size">{{ row.kb | kb }}</span>
          </li>
        {% endfor %}
      </ul>
    {% else %}
      <p class="health-ok">Every media file sits beside a document that shows it.</p>
    {% endif %}
  </section>

  {# ── Unhashed files ───────────────────────────────────────────────────── #}
  {% set sev = report.severity.checksums %}
  <section class="health-section" id="checksums">
    <h2 class="health-section__title{{ ' health-section__title--' + sev.level if health.checksums.length }}">
      {% if health.checksums.length %}<span class="health-mark">{{ sev.mark }}</span>{% endif %}Unhashed files
    </h2>
    {% if health.checksums.length %}
      <p class="health-section__lede">
        These files are still listed and still download, but the SHA-256 and
        SHA-512 shown beside every other file are missing from them: the file
        could not be read. Check its permissions.
      </p>
      <ul class="health-list health-list--compact">
        {% for row in health.checksums %}
          <li class="health-item health-item--{{ sev.level }}">
            {{ findingPath(row.file, row.url, page.url) }}
          </li>
        {% endfor %}
      </ul>
    {% else %}
      <p class="health-ok">Every file in a Files list carries both checksums.</p>
    {% endif %}
  </section>

  {# ── Oversized thumbnails ─────────────────────────────────────────────── #}
  {% set sev = report.severity.oversized %}
  <section class="health-section" id="oversized">
    <h2 class="health-section__title{{ ' health-section__title--' + sev.level if health.oversized.length }}">
      {% if health.oversized.length %}<span class="health-mark">{{ sev.mark }}</span>{% endif %}Oversized thumbnails
    </h2>
    {% if health.oversized.length %}
      <p class="health-section__lede">
        A thumbnail loads on every card that shows it, so the cost is paid many
        times over. Budget is {{ library.thumbnailBudgetKb }} KB.
      </p>
      <p class="health-section__lede">
        A row marked <em>picked</em> is not a <code>thumbnail.*</code> you chose:
        it is the smallest image in that folder, used as the card image because
        nothing there was inside the budget. Add a small
        <code>thumbnail.jpg</code> to the folder and it will be used instead.
      </p>
      <ul class="health-list health-list--compact">
        {% for row in health.oversized %}
          <li class="health-item health-item--{{ sev.level }}">
            {{ findingPath(row.file, row.url, page.url) }}
            <span class="health-item__note">{{ "picked" if row.picked else "thumbnail" }}</span>
            <span class="health-item__size health-item__size--over">{{ row.kb | kb }}</span>
          </li>
        {% endfor %}
      </ul>
    {% else %}
      <p class="health-ok">All thumbnails are within budget.</p>
    {% endif %}
  </section>

  {# ── Duplicate titles ─────────────────────────────────────────────────── #}
  {% set sev = report.severity.duplicates %}
  <section class="health-section" id="duplicates">
    <h2 class="health-section__title{{ ' health-section__title--' + sev.level if health.duplicates.length }}">
      {% if health.duplicates.length %}<span class="health-mark">{{ sev.mark }}</span>{% endif %}Duplicate titles
    </h2>
    {% if health.duplicates.length %}
      <p class="health-section__lede">
        Not a problem for the build — each keeps its own URL — but they are
        indistinguishable in the index.
      </p>
      <ul class="health-list">
        {% for row in health.duplicates %}
          <li class="health-item health-item--{{ sev.level }}">
            <span class="health-item__title">{{ row.title }}</span>
            <ul class="health-item__detail">
              {% for p in row.paths %}<li>{{ findingPath(p.path, p.url, page.url) }}</li>{% endfor %}
            </ul>
          </li>
        {% endfor %}
      </ul>
    {% else %}
      <p class="health-ok">Every title is unique.</p>
    {% endif %}
  </section>

  {# ── Renamed URLs ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────── #}
  {% set sev = report.severity.collisions %}
  <section class="health-section" id="collisions">
    <h2 class="health-section__title{{ ' health-section__title--' + sev.level if health.collisions.length }}">
      {% if health.collisions.length %}<span class="health-mark">{{ sev.mark }}</span>{% endif %}Renamed URLs
    </h2>
    {% if health.collisions.length %}
      <p class="health-section__lede">
        These wanted a URL the generator had already given to something else —
        another file in the same folder, or a page the site writes itself
        (a folder's own <code>index.html</code>, or its <code>category/</code>
        and <code>tag/</code> pages). They were renamed rather than allowed to
        collide, since a duplicate URL stops the build outright. Every link
        works; it just no longer matches the name on disk.
      </p>
      <ul class="health-list health-list--compact">
        {% for row in health.collisions %}
          <li class="health-item health-item--{{ sev.level }}">
            {{ findingPath(row.path, row.url, page.url) }}
            <span class="health-item__note">
              → {{ row.slug }}{{ "/" if row.kind == "folder" else ".html" }}
            </span>
          </li>
        {% endfor %}
      </ul>
    {% else %}
      <p class="health-ok">No filename produced a colliding URL.</p>
    {% endif %}
  </section>

  {# ── Facet spellings ──────────────────────────────────────────────────── #}
  {% set sev = report.severity.facets %}
  <section class="health-section" id="facets">
    <h2 class="health-section__title{{ ' health-section__title--' + sev.level if health.facets.length }}">
      {% if health.facets.length %}<span class="health-mark">{{ sev.mark }}</span>{% endif %}Tag spellings
    </h2>
    {% if health.facets.length %}
      <p class="health-section__lede">
        These spellings share one URL, so they have been merged onto a single
        page under the most-used version. The site works; the files are still
        inconsistent, and only you can say which spelling is the right one.
      </p>
      <ul class="health-list">
        {% for row in health.facets %}
          <li class="health-item health-item--{{ sev.level }}">
            <span class="health-field">{{ row.kind }}</span>
            <span class="health-item__title">{{ row.kept }}</span>
            {{ findingPath(row.path, row.url, page.url) }}
            <ul class="health-item__detail">
              <li>{{ row.variants | join(" · ") }}</li>
            </ul>
          </li>
        {% endfor %}
      </ul>
    {% else %}
      <p class="health-ok">Every tag and category is spelled one way.</p>
    {% endif %}
  </section>

  {# ── Math ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── #}
  {% set sev = report.severity.math %}
  <section class="health-section" id="math">
    <h2 class="health-section__title{{ ' health-section__title--' + sev.level if health.math.length }}">
      {% if health.math.length %}<span class="health-mark">{{ sev.mark }}</span>{% endif %}Math errors
    </h2>
    {% if health.math.length %}
      <p class="health-section__lede">
        These render in red on the page rather than breaking it.
      </p>
      <ul class="health-list">
        {% for row in health.math %}
          <li class="health-item health-item--{{ sev.level }}">
            {{ findingPath(row.file, row.url, page.url) }}
            <code class="health-item__tex">{{ row.tex }}</code>
            <span class="health-item__note">{{ row.message }}</span>
          </li>
        {% endfor %}
      </ul>
    {% else %}
      <p class="health-ok">All math parsed cleanly.</p>
    {% endif %}
  </section>

  {# ── Demoted headings ─────────────────────────────────────────────────── #}
  {% set sev = report.severity.demoted %}
  {% set demoted = health.demoted or [] %}
  <section class="health-section" id="demoted">
    <h2 class="health-section__title{{ ' health-section__title--' + sev.level if demoted.length }}">
      {% if demoted.length %}<span class="health-mark">{{ sev.mark }}</span>{% endif %}Demoted headings
    </h2>
    {% if demoted.length %}
      <p class="health-section__lede">
        The page already prints its title as the top heading, so an
        <code>h1</code> in the body would be a second one. These were shifted
        down a level, along with the headings under them. Nothing was lost — but
        where the two titles disagree, only you can say which is right.
      </p>
      <ul class="health-list">
        {% for row in demoted %}
          <li class="health-item health-item--{{ sev.level }}">
            {{ findingPath(row.file, "", page.url) }}
            <span class="health-item__note">
              {{ row.headings | join(" · ") }}
            </span>
          </li>
        {% endfor %}
      </ul>
    {% else %}
      <p class="health-ok">No document defines a second top-level heading.</p>
    {% endif %}
  </section>

  {# ── Generator tests ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     Everything above is about the content. This is about the generator that
     built it: the result of the last `npm test`, read from test/results.json
     rather than run, because the build must not depend on Node being present.
  #}
  {% set tests = library.tests %}
  <section class="health-section" id="tests">
    <h2 class="health-section__title{{ ' health-section__title--high' if tests.state == 'fail' }}{{ ' health-section__title--medium' if tests.state != 'pass' and tests.state != 'fail' }}">
      {% if tests.state != 'pass' %}<span class="health-mark">{{ '!' if tests.state == 'fail' else '?' }}</span>{% endif %}Generator tests
    </h2>

    {% if tests.state == 'missing' %}
      <p class="health-section__lede">
        The test suite has not been run here. Run <code>npm test</code> to check
        the generator itself — slug and URL building, front matter parsing,
        heading levels and the metadata fallbacks.
      </p>
    {% elif tests.state == 'fail' %}
      <p class="health-section__lede">
        {{ tests.fail }} of {{ tests.total }} tests failed. The pages below were
        still built, but a failing generator test means some of them may be
        wrong in a way this report cannot see.
      </p>
      <ul class="health-list">
        {% for row in tests.failures %}
          <li class="health-item health-item--high">
            {{ findingPath(row.file, "", page.url) }}
            <span class="health-item__note">{{ row.name }} — {{ row.message }}</span>
          </li>
        {% endfor %}
      </ul>
    {% elif tests.state == 'stale' %}
      <p class="health-section__lede">
        All {{ tests.pass }} tests passed when they were last run, but
        <code>lib/</code> has changed since. Run <code>npm test</code> again —
        a green tick from before the last edit says nothing about this build.
      </p>
    {% else %}
      <p class="health-ok">
        All {{ tests.pass }} generator tests passed, across
        {{ tests.files | length }} files.
      </p>
    {% endif %}
  </section>

  <p class="health-footnote">
    Everything above is about <code>input_markdown/</code>. The About and Legal
    pages are site chrome and are counted but not diagnosed here.
    Broken links, wrong-cased paths and image size budgets are checked separately,
    against the built HTML — run <code>./healthcheck.sh</code> or
    <code>healthcheck.bat</code>.
  </p>

</div>
